<article class="env-remark" id="0BX9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BX9">Remark <span data-tag="0BX9">29.50.13</span>.</a> Here is a generalization of the category of irreducible schemes and dominant rational maps. For a scheme $X$ denote $X^0$ the set of points $x \in X$ with $\dim (\mathcal{O}_{X, x}) = 0$, in other words, $X^0$ is the set of generic points of irreducible components of $X$. Then we can consider the category with </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
objects are schemes $X$ such that every quasi-compact open has finitely many irreducible components, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
morphisms from $X$ to $Y$ are rational maps $f : U \to Y$ from $X$ to $Y$ such that $f(U^0) = Y^0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> If $U \subset X$ is a dense open of a scheme, then $U^0 \subset X^0$ need not be an equality, but if $X$ is an object of our category, then this is the case. Thus given two morphisms in our category, the composition is well defined and a morphism in our category. </p>
</article>